          "thm-real-complex-clifford-algebra-classification"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "M. F. Atiyah, R. Bott, and A. Shapiro, Clifford Modules, Part I",
              "url": "https://www.math.ens.psl.eu/~benoist/refs/ABS.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin Wernli, Lecture Notes on Spin Geometry, §§1.5.1–1.5.4, pp. 26–33",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1911.09766"
            },
            {
              "title": "Jean Gallier, Clifford Algebras, Clifford Groups, and a Generalization of the Quaternions: The Pin and Spin Groups, Chapter 1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0805.0311"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $(V,B)$ be a finite-dimensional nondegenerate real quadratic space. The real linear span of $\\operatorname{Spin}_{\\mathrm{full}}(V,Q)$ inside $\\mathrm{Cl}(V,Q)$ is $\\mathrm{Cl}^0(V,Q)$. In particular, a subspace of a Clifford module is Spin-invariant exactly when it is a $\\mathrm{Cl}^0$-submodule, and the two commutants agree. Here a **Clifford spinor representation** of full-even Spin means the restriction of a finite-dimensional unital $\\mathrm{Cl}^0(V,Q)$-module. This does not mean an arbitrary group representation of Spin. Use the PA-04 even-Clifford classification in [A1] and [L1]. If\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cl}^0(V,Q)\\cong\\prod_{j=1}^s M_{r_j}(D_j),\\qquad D_j\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C,\\mathbb H\\},$$\n\nthen the irreducible finite-dimensional real Clifford spinor representations are precisely one column module $D_j^{r_j}$ for each factor. Its real dimension is $r_j\\dim_{\\mathbb R}D_j$ and its commuting endomorphism algebra is $D_j^{\\mathrm{op}}$, isomorphic to $D_j$. Distinct factors, including split factors, give inequivalent Spin modules. The module type is determined by the even algebra, not automatically by the full Clifford algebra.\n\nIn dimensions $0$ and $1$, the even Clifford algebra is $\\mathbb R$ and there is one one-dimensional real Clifford spinor representation. In dimension $2$, definite signatures have even algebra $\\mathbb C$ and one two-dimensional real Clifford spinor representation of complex type; signature $(1,1)$ has even algebra $\\mathbb R\\oplus\\mathbb R$ and two inequivalent one-dimensional real Clifford spinor representations.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
